# 04.asm – Stack Frame in 32-bit Assembly

This program demonstrates how a function uses the stack to store local variables in 32-bit assembly.

### How the program works

The program starts by calling the `work` function.

call work


CALL saves the return address on the stack and jumps to `work`. ESP decreases by 4 bytes because this is a 32-bit program.

### Creating the stack frame


push ebp
mov ebp, esp
sub esp, 8


- push ebp saves the previous base pointer and decreases ESP by 4 bytes.
- mov ebp, esp sets EBP as the reference point for the stack frame.
- sub esp, 8 reserves 8 bytes for two local variables.

### Storing and retrieving values


mov dword [ebp - 4], 100
mov dword [ebp - 8], 200


The program stores 100 and 200 in the reserved stack space.


mov eax, [ebp - 4]
mov ebx, [ebp - 8]


The values are retrieved into EAX and EBX respectively. ESP does not change during these operations. NOP also has no effect on the stack.

### Restoring the stack

mov esp, ebp
pop ebp
ret


- mov esp, ebp removes the space reserved for local variables.
- pop ebp restores the previous base pointer and increases ESP by 4 bytes.
- ret retrieves the return address and returns execution to the instruction after CALL. ESP increases by another 4 bytes.

The program then exits using the Linux 32-bit exit system call (`int 0x80`).

### ESP changes

Assuming the initial ESP value is X:

| Instruction | ESP |
|---|---|
| Program starts | X |
| CALL work | X - 4 |
| PUSH EBP | X- 8 |
| MOV EBP, ESP | X - 8 |
| SUB ESP, 8 | X - 16 |
| Store and retrieve values | X - 16 |
| MOV ESP, EBP | X - 8 |
| POP EBP | X - 4 |
| RET | X |

### Observations

- CALL saves the return address on the stack.
- PUSH EBP saves the previous base pointer.
- EBP is used to access local variables.
- ESP decreases when space is reserved for local variables.
- POP EBP restores the previous base pointer.
- RET returns execution to the instruction after CALL.
- ESP returns to its original position.
- The program produces no screen output.

### Registers changes

- ESP changes as the function saves the return address, saves EBP, reserves local variable space and restores the stack.
- EBP stores the base address of the function's stack frame and is restored before returning.
- EAX receives the value 100 from `[ebp - 4]`.
- EBX receives the value 200 from `[ebp - 8]`.
- EAX is later changed to 1 for the 32-bit exit system call.
- EBX is set to 0 to indicate successful program termination.